# Frontend (React)

> Paths below are relative to `apps/desktop/`. The landing site is documented in [LANDING.md](LANDING.md).

React + TypeScript UI for the Orbit desktop launcher. Runs inside Tauri webview (production)
or Vite dev server with mock Tauri API (browser dev).

Product scope, screens, and Tauri commands: [docs/DESIGN_DOC.mdc](DESIGN_DOC.mdc).
Themes and visual language: [docs/UI.mdc](UI.mdc).

## Responsibilities

- Render controller-first, fullscreen game launcher UI with theme-specific layouts.
- Manage screen navigation via Zustand (`ui.store.ts`) — no React Router.
- Invoke all backend operations through `src/lib/tauri.ts`.
- Implement spatial focus + gamepad navigation (`controllers/`).
- Cache library data with TanStack Query (`hooks/useGames.ts`).
- Apply user themes (built-in + custom JSON) via CSS variables.

## Tech stack

- React 19
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Tailwind CSS 3
- Zustand (UI + theme + session state)
- TanStack Query (game list/detail fetching)
- Framer Motion (screen transitions)
- Lucide React (icons)
- Playwright (E2E)

## Platforms

| Surface | Notes |
| ------- | ----- |
| **Tauri desktop** | Primary target — full command surface, native dialogs, window hide/show |
| **Vite dev (browser)** | Mock API via `devMockApi.ts` for UI iteration and Playwright |

## UI guidelines

- **Controller-first:** every screen must be fully navigable with gamepad; mouse is secondary.
- **Couch distance:** large type, high contrast, minimal chrome; scrollbars hidden globally.
- **Fullscreen default:** `useFullscreen` requests fullscreen on launch.
- **Cursor auto-hide:** hides after controller activity (`useCursorAutoHide`).
- **Focus rings:** `[data-focused="true"]` styling driven by `FocusManager` + `Focusable`.
- **No direct launch:** never spawn processes from TS — always `launchGame()` via Tauri.
- **Library grid:** `GameCard` is `React.memo`'d with stable store handlers; full window virtualization is intentionally skipped so FocusManager spatial nav keeps all grid nodes mounted.

## Screen map

Screens are selected in `App.tsx` from `useUiStore((s) => s.screen)`:

| Screen | Component | Module folder |
| ------ | --------- | -------------- |
| Home | `HomePage` → `MacosHome` | `modules/home/`, `themes/` |
| Library | `LibraryPage` | `modules/library/` |
| Search | `SearchPage` | `modules/search/` |
| Game details | `GameDetailsPage` | `modules/game-details/` |
| Edit | `GameEditPage` | `modules/game-details/` — Fetch metadata & artwork |
| Add game | `AddGamePage` | `modules/imports/` |
| Settings | `SettingsPage` | `modules/settings/` — themes + theme studio, TheGamesDB/SteamGridDB keys, auto-enrich |

Every theme renders the single Liquid Glass shell (`themes/MacosShell.tsx`) with its original styling. Game themes change the actual SteamGridDB image logo and hero background, not the palette or layout. `theme.store.ts` loads both assets on explicit game-theme selection and guards against stale asynchronous results. Errors leave the previous selection active. Retired platform/color IDs resolve to Liquid Glass. Custom files remain readable, with legacy text logos suppressed. `useActiveTheme()` (`hooks/useActiveTheme.ts`) returns the active `ThemeDefinition`. `focusRestoreId(screen, gameId)` and `screenEntryFocusId(screen)` in `lib/navigation.ts` resolve controller focus targets for the shell.

## Module pattern

Each module is self-contained under `src/modules/<name>/` with colocated components and hooks.
Shared cross-module UI lives in `src/components/`.

```
